WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:10.640
The warriors arrived on May 1, 1763. Leading&nbsp;
them was the Odawa chief Pontiac. The commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:19.440
of Fort Detroit, Major Henry Gladwin, received&nbsp;
them warmly. When they left he suspected they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:27.840
were up to no good. Ten days later, his fears&nbsp;
were confirmed: Pontiac attacked Fort Detroit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:32.560
This marked the beginning of a siege that had&nbsp;
little in common with the positional warfare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:38.800
typical of European sieges but instead followed&nbsp;
the logic of frontier warfare. Warriors moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:44.320
through the forests, brutal ambushes,&nbsp;
treacherous stratagems, swift maneuvers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:50.080
torture at the stake, and even the use of&nbsp;
fire ships all characterized the fighting.&nbsp;

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:55.440
The attack on Fort Detroit marked the beginning of&nbsp;
a wave that swept across the British settlements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:01.840
and forts of the Great Lakes region. During the&nbsp;
conflict known as Pontiac’s War, numerous tribes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:07.680
rose up against the British in an effort to drive&nbsp;
soldiers and settlers from the region. Within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:13.760
a matter of weeks, they raided dozens of farms&nbsp;
and villages, expelled hundreds of settlers, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:19.840
captured nine British forts. This is how modern&nbsp;
historiography tells the story of the siege of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:26.080
Fort Detroit and the beginning of Pontiac’s War.
