Chapter 1: The Path to War&nbsp;

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:06.080
In the early 1760s, a prophet traveled through the&nbsp;
villages of the Ohio Valley. His name was Neolin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:10.560
and he belonged to the Delaware tribe.&nbsp;
He preached that the Native Americans had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:15.600
angered the Great Spirit by allowing themselves&nbsp;
to be corrupted by the European way of life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:20.720
and that this was the cause of the wars,&nbsp;
epidemics, and crop failures they had suffered in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:26.800
recent years. To regain the Great Spirit’s favor,&nbsp;
they would have to turn away from the Europeans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:33.600
return to their traditional ways, and renounce&nbsp;
white trade goods, alcohol, and European weapons.&nbsp;

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:38.960
Neolin’s teachings found a receptive audience&nbsp;
because many Native Americans blamed the crisis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:44.480
they faced on the Anglo-Americans, who&nbsp;
were pushing ever farther west. Soon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:49.200
his message began to shape the spiritual&nbsp;
life of numerous tribes and alter the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:54.240
political climate. Discontent spread&nbsp;
among the tribes of the region.&nbsp;

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:59.440
The British themselves played a major role&nbsp;
in bringing about this shift in sentiment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:04.880
Jeffery Amherst, the British commander-in-chief&nbsp;
in North America, pursued a hardline policy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:09.840
toward the native American population. Since&nbsp;
conquering the French colony of New France&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:16.000
in 1760, British forces had steadily expanded&nbsp;
their control over the occupied territories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:22.000
particularly in the Great Lakes region,&nbsp;
known at the time as the Pays d’en Haut.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:26.960
Although they had promised in the Treaty of&nbsp;
Easton only a few years earlier to remain for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:32.640
no longer than necessary, they expanded their&nbsp;
forts and trading posts across the frontier and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:39.040
sent an ever-growing stream of settlers westward.
Before the arrival of the British, the French had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:44.480
generally treated their native American allies&nbsp;
with respect. They traded with them, exchanged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:51.200
gifts, and largely stayed out of their internal&nbsp;
affairs. The British took a different approach.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:56.400
Many believed that the native American peoples&nbsp;
should submit to British rule. Amherst sought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:02.560
to end the practice of gift-giving, insisted that&nbsp;
trade be conducted at fixed prices, and regarded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:08.480
it as the natural right of the British to settle&nbsp;
the conquered territories and cultivate the land.&nbsp;

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:14.400
The conduct of the British so deeply offended the&nbsp;
native American population that many came to long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:20.640
for the return of the French. However, Jefferey&nbsp;
Amherst did not concern himself with this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:28.880
as he believed these “savages” could pose no real&nbsp;
threat to his soldiers. To be on the safe side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:34.080
he restricted the sale of weapons and gunpowder&nbsp;
to them in order to limit their capabilities in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:39.440
the event of an uprising—without considering&nbsp;
that these weapons were also essential for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:46.640
hunting in peacetime. The native Americans saw&nbsp;
this—rightly so—as a clear sign that the British&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:52.240
did not view them as equal partners&nbsp;
but instead sought to subjugate them.&nbsp;

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:58.800
One man was particularly troubled by Amherst’s&nbsp;
policies: the Odawa chief Pontiac, who held&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:05.760
great influence among the tribes along the Detroit&nbsp;
River. In the autumn of 1762, he began advocating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:12.480
armed resistance–as did many other chiefs.
Ironically, the spark that finally set the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:18.240
rebellion in motion was the news of the end of&nbsp;
the war between France and Great Britain. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:24.400
the British announced in 1763 that the Seven&nbsp;
Years’ War—and with it the struggle for North&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:30.640
America—had come to an end, many chiefs&nbsp;
regarded the announcement with suspicion.&nbsp;

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:36.720
At the remote outposts of the frontier, there&nbsp;
were, of course, no official documents to confirm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:42.880
that the treaty was real. As a result, many native&nbsp;
Americans believed the British had simply invented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:48.720
the peace to conceal a French counteroffensive&nbsp;
and prevent them from siding with the French.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:55.280
In reality, there was no counteroffensive—but&nbsp;
the hope that their old allies might return was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.280 --> 00:07:04.960
enough for many chiefs to take up the hatchet.
On April 27, 1764, Pontiac announced at a war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:12.400
council of the Odawa, Potawatomi, and Wyandot on&nbsp;
the Ecorse River that he would attack Detroit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:17.680
The fort was not only located in the heart of&nbsp;
their territory, but also served as the hub&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:24.160
of the British outposts between the Wabash and&nbsp;
Michilimackinac. Only Fort Pitt and Fort Niagara&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:30.720
were of comparable importance. Pontiac delivered&nbsp;
a fiery speech, voicing his anger over British&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:36.800
treatment of his people, and finally presented&nbsp;
the other chiefs with a wampum belt that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:42.320
King of France had allegedly sent him as a&nbsp;
sign that he would return. When he finished,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:51.200
loud cries of approval broke out.
Chapter 2: War by Stratagem&nbsp;

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:56.640
It was a warm Sunday afternoon in early&nbsp;
May when Pontiac arrived at Fort Detroit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:04.080
with fifty warriors. The fort’s commander, Major&nbsp;
Henry Gladwin, awaited them at the eastern gate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:10.400
greeted them warmly, and invited them inside.&nbsp;
Pontiac explained that they had come to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:16.000
strengthen their relations and underscored his&nbsp;
good intentions by performing a peace dance with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:23.040
several of his companions. The remaining warriors&nbsp;
used the time to walk through the fort—and to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:30.320
scout it out. The walls of the complex consisted&nbsp;
of four-meter-high palisades with a parapet walk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:37.200
and three projecting bastions extended the&nbsp;
garrison’s field of fire. Fort Detroit was strong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:47.920
but the guards were few, the atmosphere&nbsp;
was relaxed, and the gates stood wide open.&nbsp;

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:53.600
What Pontiac’s scouts had seen was so promising&nbsp;
that the chief convened another war council the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.600 --> 00:09:00.640
very next day. The allies gathered in a Potawatomi&nbsp;
village. To ensure the British learned nothing of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:07.280
their intentions, they sent all women and children&nbsp;
away and posted guards. Pontiac then laid out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:17.600
his plan to the other chiefs: he intended to&nbsp;
capture Fort Detroit by a stratagem of war.&nbsp;

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:23.920
On the morning of May 7, the Potawatomi and&nbsp;
Wyandot surrounded the fort. They took up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:29.920
positions where they remained well hidden but&nbsp;
could intercept any reinforcements—and anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:36.880
attempting to flee would run straight into them.&nbsp;
Pontiac then returned to the fort with sixty Odawa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:44.160
warriors, acting completely at ease, as if it&nbsp;
were just another friendly visit. Their weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:50.880
were carefully concealed beneath their coats.
When Pontiac entered the fort, he was astonished.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:57.200
The garrison was apparently not as unsuspecting&nbsp;
as he had assumed. The fort’s two schooners were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.200 --> 00:10:02.720
patrolling the river, and at the gates there&nbsp;
were at least twice as many guards as during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:10.240
his last visit. Nevertheless, the chief continued&nbsp;
on—only to discover that cannons were trained on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:17.440
key positions within the fort and, finally, to&nbsp;
come upon a large contingent of Redcoats under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:24.480
arms on the parade ground. Gladwin greeted the&nbsp;
chief warmly once more, but the menacing barrels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:32.400
of his men made their message unmistakably clear.
Major Henry Gladwin was a veteran who had earned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:38.080
his stripes in eight years of war against the&nbsp;
French. He had been in command of Fort Detroit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:45.520
and its 120-man garrison since the summer of&nbsp;
1761. Over those three years, he had built up an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:51.440
extensive network—and it was this network that&nbsp;
allowed him, despite Pontiac’s precautions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.440 --> 00:10:58.640
to learn of the impending attack[RS1.1].
Since Pontiac could not risk a direct attack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:04.640
he tried to keep up his ruse the next day. He&nbsp;
returned to Gladwin, smoked a peace pipe with him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:08.960
and announced that his warriors wished to pay&nbsp;
their respects to the garrison to prove their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:15.200
peaceful intentions. When they returned on the&nbsp;
morning of May 9, they found the gates locked.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:21.360
Gladwin had seen through the deception. Pontiac&nbsp;
now openly declared war. He stated that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:40.604
British “might stay in our Fort” but his warriors&nbsp;
“would keep the country.” The siege had begun.&nbsp;

00:11:40.604 --> 00:11:42.960
Chapter 3: The Siege of Fort Detroit
Mrs. Turnbull and her two sons were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:48.080
working in the field in front of their small&nbsp;
farm when Pontiac’s men appeared. They were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.080 --> 00:11:54.160
the first victims. Then came two soldiers who&nbsp;
were guarding a herd of cattle on Hog Island,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.160 --> 00:12:01.760
followed by dozens more Anglo-American settlers.&nbsp;
Pontiac was putting his threat into action. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:07.520
occupied the land in order to intimidate the&nbsp;
British and pressure them into withdrawing.&nbsp;

00:12:07.520 --> 00:12:13.760
The defenders’ situation was precarious. For the&nbsp;
moment, they were protected by the fort’s sturdy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.760 --> 00:12:19.520
palisades, but an inspection revealed that&nbsp;
their supplies would last no more than three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:26.160
weeks on half rations, and they were too few&nbsp;
to defend the fort in the long term. Therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:31.920
the men were relieved when, the next day, a&nbsp;
group of unarmed Wyandot appeared beneath the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:38.400
walls and proposed a ceasefire. Gladwin was&nbsp;
suspicious, but he allowed his predecessor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:44.800
Donald Campbell, and Lieutenant George McDougall&nbsp;
to go out and meet Pontiac for negotiations.&nbsp;

00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:48.640
The meeting took place at the home of&nbsp;
a relative of the fort’s last French&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:55.520
commander. It had barely begun when Pontiac broke&nbsp;
the truce, had the two officers seized and bound,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.520 --> 00:13:00.400
and told Gladwin that he would not release them&nbsp;
until the British left the fort and retreated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.400 --> 00:13:05.520
eastward over the mountains. Gladwin, who had&nbsp;
used the lull in the fighting to bring supplies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:10.640
into the fort, refused to be blackmailed.&nbsp;
He declared that he would not negotiate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:16.800
until Pontiac released the hostages.
So, the fighting began anew. Soon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:22.480
warriors swarmed around the fort. They attacked&nbsp;
anyone who left the protection of the ramparts and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:28.080
fired from the cover of sheds and houses at the&nbsp;
guards on the palisade. In the first few weeks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:33.440
they killed twenty Redcoats and captured&nbsp;
fifteen more. In response, Gladwin launched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:39.120
several sorties, destroyed everything that could&nbsp;
provide cover for the attackers, and positioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:44.960
two cannons to take advantage of the clear line&nbsp;
of fire. After the first shots from the guns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.960 --> 00:13:51.120
the warriors quickly learned to be more cautious.
In the meantime, attacks had also begun across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:56.320
the rest of the Great Lakes region.&nbsp;
As if following a coordinated plan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.320 --> 00:14:02.320
groups of native Americans simultaneously raided&nbsp;
remote British outposts. The first to fall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.320 --> 00:14:10.160
on May 16, was the small Fort Sandusky on Lake&nbsp;
Erie, followed by Fort Saint Joseph, Fort Miami,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.160 --> 00:14:18.080
and Fort Ouiatenon. All five were taken the&nbsp;
native American way: by a stratagem of war. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:26.080
same fate then befell the first major fortress.
On June 2, a group of Ojibwa were playing lacrosse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.080 --> 00:14:31.520
at their camp near Fort Michilimackinac.&nbsp;
The soldiers of the garrison gathered in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.520 --> 00:14:36.080
the shade of the palisades to watch. Soon&nbsp;
they were so engrossed that they did not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:43.360
notice several Indigenous women slipping quietly&nbsp;
through the gate. Then, suddenly, the ball was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.360 --> 00:14:50.320
thrown into the fort, seemingly by accident, and&nbsp;
the players rushed after it—not to retrieve it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.320 --> 00:14:56.000
but to seize the weapons the women had smuggled&nbsp;
in beneath their clothing. Within seconds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.000 --> 00:15:00.640
the game turned deadly. The Ojibwa&nbsp;
surrounded the surprised Redcoats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.640 --> 00:15:07.120
overwhelmed them, and nearly wiped them out.
A little further west, in the Ohio Valley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.120 --> 00:15:14.000
the Delaware and Shawnee attacked Fort Ligonier&nbsp;
and then encircled Fort Pitt, while in the east&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:21.600
the Seneca overran Forts Venango, Le Boeuf, and&nbsp;
Presque Isle, and threatened Fort Niagara. Soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.600 --> 00:15:35.120
the entire region was in flames.
Chapter 4: Frontier Warfare&nbsp;

00:15:35.120 --> 00:15:40.720
Pontiac was frustrated. The cannons of&nbsp;
Fort Detroit kept his warriors at bay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.720 --> 00:15:45.680
support from the other tribes was slow in&nbsp;
coming, and the hoped-for alliance with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.680 --> 00:15:52.080
French settlers in the area failed to materialize.&nbsp;
They hesitated to side with Pontiac because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.080 --> 00:15:59.120
were unsure whether a French army had actually&nbsp;
landed in America, as the chief had claimed.&nbsp;

00:15:59.120 --> 00:16:05.520
Nevertheless, the besiegers worked tirelessly&nbsp;
to isolate the fort. The Wyandot and Potawatomi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:10.480
patrolled the Detroit River, while two hundred&nbsp;
warriors in canoes guarded the river’s mouth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.480 --> 00:16:17.520
where it flows into Lake Erie. In mid-May, they&nbsp;
intercepted a trader with five heavily laden boats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.520 --> 00:16:23.360
carrying goods, including seventeen barrels&nbsp;
of gunpowder. When Gladwin learned of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.360 --> 00:16:28.160
he stationed one of his two schooners,&nbsp;
the Huron, at the river mouth to prevent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:33.520
such incidents from happening again.
Pontiac’s warriors soon noticed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.520 --> 00:16:39.360
there were only six soldiers aboard the ship&nbsp;
and decided to capture the schooner. As they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.360 --> 00:16:44.640
approached in their canoes, however, they&nbsp;
realized that the firepower of the larger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.640 --> 00:16:50.720
vessel more than compensated for their numerical&nbsp;
advantage. In their light boats, they stood no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.720 --> 00:16:57.280
chance against the Huron’s six cannons.
The next day, Pontiac tried a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.280 --> 00:17:03.840
approach. He sent Campbell in a canoe to the Huron&nbsp;
to tell the crew to come ashore to negotiate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.840 --> 00:17:09.680
The prisoner, however, saw through the plan and&nbsp;
gave the sailors a discreet warning that it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.680 --> 00:17:17.120
a trap. Instead of landing, the Huron set sail&nbsp;
and headed for Niagara to fetch reinforcements.&nbsp;

00:17:17.120 --> 00:17:22.080
As the Wyandot and Potawatomi followed the&nbsp;
schooner along the coast, they came upon a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.080 --> 00:17:28.080
British camp in the woods near Point Pelee. It&nbsp;
was the camp of Lieutenant Cornelius Cuyler and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.080 --> 00:17:34.640
his company of Queens Rangers. They were on their&nbsp;
way to Fort Detroit but were still unaware of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.640 --> 00:17:40.560
siege. By the time Cuyler realized they&nbsp;
were in danger, it was already too late.&nbsp;

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:47.760
A man came running out of the woods, screaming,&nbsp;
his face contorted with fear. Cuyler had barely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:53.200
gathered from his confused account that his&nbsp;
companions had been scalped when, suddenly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.200 --> 00:17:59.360
warriors stormed the British camp from all&nbsp;
directions. The Rangers ran for their lives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:59.360 --> 00:18:06.240
but only about thirty-five reached the boats; the&nbsp;
remaining sixty were surrounded. Those who fell in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:12.960
the melee were, in a sense, the fortunate ones.&nbsp;
The prisoners were dragged to Pontiac’s camp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.960 --> 00:18:20.400
where they were tortured, used as practice&nbsp;
targets for the boys, or burned at the stake.&nbsp;

00:18:20.400 --> 00:18:25.680
The mistreatment of the prisoners had&nbsp;
repercussions. On June 18, chiefs from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.680 --> 00:18:31.680
the Ojibwa and Shawnee arrived at the Detroit&nbsp;
River to consult with Pontiac. They were less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.680 --> 00:18:38.240
than enthusiastic about his actions. He had made&nbsp;
little progress, acted with unnecessary brutality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.240 --> 00:18:45.040
and taken food from the French settlers—whom they&nbsp;
hoped to win over—to supply his warriors. Faced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:51.200
with this open criticism, Pontiac realized&nbsp;
that his authority was being challenged.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.200 --> 00:18:56.640
When the returning Huron was sighted at the&nbsp;
mouth of the Detroit River on June 21, he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.640 --> 00:19:02.000
decided to restore his prestige. He&nbsp;
had his warriors prepare an ambush.&nbsp;

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:08.960
As the Huron passed Turkey Island, gunshots&nbsp;
suddenly rang out, accompanied by loud war cries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.960 --> 00:19:14.240
The helmsman steered the ship into the middle&nbsp;
of the river to escape the fire from the island,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:14.240 --> 00:19:19.520
but at the same time dozens of canoes&nbsp;
surged out from behind the small islet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.520 --> 00:19:25.520
The light boats closed in at lightning&nbsp;
speed—but the Huron was prepared. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.520 --> 00:19:31.760
the attackers were just a stone’s throw away,&nbsp;
the captain gave a signal. Three dozen soldiers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.760 --> 00:19:37.280
who had been hiding below deck leapt to&nbsp;
the rail and opened fire. It was Cuyler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.280 --> 00:19:43.840
and the remnants of his ranger company, whom the&nbsp;
Huron had taken aboard en route. The unexpected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.840 --> 00:19:48.640
reinforcements unleashed a sudden burst of&nbsp;
firepower that drove the attackers back.&nbsp;

00:19:50.320 --> 00:19:55.520
The very next day, Pontiac suffered&nbsp;
another setback. Lieutenant George&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.520 --> 00:20:00.640
McDougall and three other prisoners escaped&nbsp;
from the Odawa village because the guards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.640 --> 00:20:08.640
had fallen asleep. Campbell, however, did not&nbsp;
attempt to escape, as he was rather heavyset.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.640 --> 00:20:22.960
He would come to bitterly regret it.
Chapter 6: Fire Ships on the Frontier&nbsp;

00:20:22.960 --> 00:20:27.440
When Amherst learned in New York that&nbsp;
Pontiac had attacked Fort Detroit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:27.440 --> 00:20:33.840
he could hardly believe his ears. When reports&nbsp;
of raids began to pile up, however, the general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:33.840 --> 00:20:39.120
had to admit that he had underestimated the&nbsp;
native Americans. He immediately raised two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:39.120 --> 00:20:45.680
armies to put an end to the attacks and punish&nbsp;
the rebels. Colonel Henri Bouquet advanced into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.680 --> 00:20:51.920
the Ohio Valley to relieve Fort Pitt, while&nbsp;
Captain James Dalyell set out for Detroit.&nbsp;

00:20:51.920 --> 00:20:58.560
Meanwhile, some 900 warriors had gathered at&nbsp;
Fort Detroit. That was enough for Pontiac to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.560 --> 00:21:04.320
change his tactics. Instead of continuing&nbsp;
to pick off individual guards on the walls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:04.320 --> 00:21:10.960
he established a tight blockade to starve the&nbsp;
garrison. It was a gamble. Gladwin had food&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:10.960 --> 00:21:18.480
to last several weeks thanks to the Huron, while&nbsp;
the besiegers’ supplies were already running low.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:18.480 --> 00:21:25.440
If Pontiac failed to secure provisions, his men&nbsp;
would starve before the garrison did—and, worse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:25.440 --> 00:21:31.120
they would face the winter without stores.
To prevent this, Pontiac once again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:31.120 --> 00:21:37.040
urged the French settlers to support him.&nbsp;
The response was modest. Some allowed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:37.040 --> 00:21:43.600
Indigenous warriors to grow corn in their fields,&nbsp;
about forty young frontier woodsmen, known as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:43.600 --> 00:21:49.920
coureurs des bois, joined him as volunteers, and&nbsp;
a handful of workers helped him build an earthen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:49.920 --> 00:21:55.520
redoubt near the fort. However, Pontiac’s&nbsp;
increasingly coercive demands also pushed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:55.520 --> 00:22:00.960
many French settlers to side with the British.&nbsp;
Gladwin gathered these new arrivals in the church&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.960 --> 00:22:07.680
and read them the terms of the Treaty of Paris.&nbsp;
This time, they were persuaded. What Pontiac&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:07.680 --> 00:22:16.240
and the other chiefs had claimed was false: the&nbsp;
French had not come to reclaim their colonies.&nbsp;

00:22:16.240 --> 00:22:21.920
On July 4, the garrison launched a sortie to&nbsp;
destroy the redoubt, which was almost complete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.920 --> 00:22:28.880
by now. A bloody skirmish ensued, during which&nbsp;
the son of the Ojibwa chief Wasson was killed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.880 --> 00:22:34.640
The Ojibwa were outraged and called for&nbsp;
retribution. Their anger fell upon the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:34.640 --> 00:22:41.440
defenseless Campbell. Wasson and several warriors&nbsp;
dragged him from the Odawa camp and tied him to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:41.440 --> 00:22:47.040
stake at Parent’s Creek. They tortured him,&nbsp;
tore out his heart while he was still alive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:47.040 --> 00:22:52.800
and threw his body into the creek.
Gladwin’s retaliatory strike followed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:52.800 --> 00:22:59.040
immediately. He sent his ships up the river&nbsp;
to bombard and destroy the Odawa village.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:59.040 --> 00:23:06.160
The Odawa subsequently rebuilt their huts in&nbsp;
a swamp, far from rivers and roads. The attack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:06.160 --> 00:23:11.120
made it clear to Pontiac once again that the two&nbsp;
schooners were among Gladwin’s most important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:11.120 --> 00:23:18.800
weapons. He needed to destroy them. To that end&nbsp;
he enlisted the help of the Meloche brothers, who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:18.800 --> 00:23:25.920
showed his warriors how to build fire ships. By&nbsp;
July 10, they were ready. The brothers pushed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:25.920 --> 00:23:33.520
boats out onto the Detroit River, set them ablaze,&nbsp;
and let them drift toward the two schooners. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:33.520 --> 00:23:40.960
warriors cheered as the flames flared up—and&nbsp;
lamented when the fire ships burned out before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:40.960 --> 00:23:47.760
reaching their target. Two further attempts&nbsp;
also failed, and when Gladwin reinforced several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:47.760 --> 00:23:54.880
smaller boats with fireproof plating to deflect&nbsp;
other fire ships, Pontiac abandoned the plan.&nbsp;

00:23:54.880 --> 00:24:00.640
After this setback, resolve began to waver.&nbsp;
Native American warfare did not typically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:00.640 --> 00:24:06.160
involve prolonged sieges, and as a result,&nbsp;
some chiefs were running out of patience. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:06.160 --> 00:24:12.400
Potawatomi and Wyandot even offered the garrison&nbsp;
a truce. But before they reached an agreement with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:12.400 --> 00:24:18.640
Gladwin, a messenger arrived. He reported&nbsp;
that the Delaware were besieging Fort Pitt.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:18.640 --> 00:24:31.840
War-weariness vanished into thin air.
Chapter 5: The Battle of Bloody Run&nbsp;

00:24:31.840 --> 00:24:38.080
On the morning of July 28, the guards on&nbsp;
the palisades heard gunfire. Downstream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:38.080 --> 00:24:44.240
where the Wyandot were camped, something was&nbsp;
happening. Gladwin feared that Pontiac was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:44.240 --> 00:24:51.280
finally attempting an attack and immediately sent&nbsp;
a dozen volunteers to find out what was going on.&nbsp;

00:24:51.280 --> 00:24:58.160
The men rowed downstream in a small boat, into&nbsp;
the thick morning fog. After a while, they heard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:58.160 --> 00:25:04.640
the splash of oars. The sound grew louder and&nbsp;
louder until suddenly the outline of a boat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:04.640 --> 00:25:12.640
emerged from the mist. Then a second, a third,&nbsp;
and finally a whole flotilla. But these were not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:12.640 --> 00:25:20.560
Pontiac’s canoes. As the rising sun burned away&nbsp;
the fog, the scouts made out twenty-two boats—and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:20.560 --> 00:25:28.240
on board, redcoats. Dalyell had arrived.
Dalyell was an ambitious young captain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:28.240 --> 00:25:34.720
eager to prove himself in battle. He wanted&nbsp;
to surprise Pontiac in his camp, destroy him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:34.720 --> 00:25:40.480
and use the victory to advance his career.&nbsp;
Gladwin, however, considered it naive to attempt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:40.480 --> 00:25:47.440
a surprise attack on the Odawa. He advocated&nbsp;
waiting out the situation at Detroit. The news of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:47.440 --> 00:25:53.520
the attack on Fort Pitt had certainly galvanized&nbsp;
the Indigenous forces, but he believed it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:53.520 --> 00:26:00.000
only a matter of time before they lost interest&nbsp;
in the long, monotonous siege. Although Gladwin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:06.880
was in command, Dalyell ultimately prevailed,&nbsp;
invoking his authority as Amherst’s aide-de-camp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:06.880 --> 00:26:16.320
and his status as the son of a baronet.
On July 31, at about 2:30 a.m., Dalyell set out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:16.320 --> 00:26:22.000
accompanied by the famous frontiersman Robert&nbsp;
Rogers. Two hundred and forty-seven men marched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:22.000 --> 00:26:28.320
north along the river in a double column. Two&nbsp;
boats, each equipped with swivel guns, provided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:28.320 --> 00:26:34.800
cover from the water. Near Parent’s Creek, Dalyell&nbsp;
shortened the column, formed a vanguard and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:34.800 --> 00:26:41.360
rearguard, and ordered his men to move in silence.
As the first British soldiers crossed the narrow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:41.360 --> 00:26:47.760
wooden bridge over the creek, all was&nbsp;
still quiet. But just a few steps later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:47.760 --> 00:26:54.800
arrows and gunfire rained down on them. Several&nbsp;
men were hit, including Dalyell, who was only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:54.800 --> 00:27:01.360
lightly wounded. As the vanguard attempted to&nbsp;
withdraw, they heard fighting break out behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:01.360 --> 00:27:08.880
them as well. Pontiac had set a trap. Four hundred&nbsp;
warriors had hidden in the woods along the path,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:08.880 --> 00:27:14.480
waited until the British column had passed,&nbsp;
and then struck from the flank and rear. Panic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:14.480 --> 00:27:21.440
spread. Only Robert Rogers kept his composure.&nbsp;
He led his company to a nearby farmstead and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:21.440 --> 00:27:26.560
turned it into a makeshift blockhouse.
Against the picturesque backdrop of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:26.560 --> 00:27:34.160
breaking dawn, a brutal scene unfolded. The&nbsp;
Redcoats fought bravely but in disarray. Dozens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:34.160 --> 00:27:40.960
were cut down or captured before Dalyell finally&nbsp;
regained control and rallied his men. Once order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:40.960 --> 00:27:47.440
was restored, he reformed the rearguard as the&nbsp;
vanguard and began retreating toward Detroit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:47.440 --> 00:27:54.960
While attempting to break through the attackers’&nbsp;
line, he was struck again—this time fatally.&nbsp;

00:27:54.960 --> 00:28:00.560
Command fell to the leader of the rearguard,&nbsp;
Captain Grant, who began fighting his way back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:00.560 --> 00:28:07.040
toward the fort from cover to cover. Rogers’ men&nbsp;
were the last to leave. They were only able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:07.040 --> 00:28:12.160
abandon the blockhouse once the two boats&nbsp;
provided covering fire from the river. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:12.160 --> 00:28:19.840
blood of the fallen stained Parent’s Creek red.&nbsp;
Since that day, it has been known as Bloody Run.&nbsp;

00:28:19.840 --> 00:28:26.160
The senseless Battle of Bloody Run weakened both&nbsp;
sides, and for the rest of the summer and into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:26.160 --> 00:28:32.800
fall, they focused on conserving their resources.&nbsp;
Pontiac controlled the surrounding countryside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:32.800 --> 00:28:39.600
while the garrison remained behind its palisades.&nbsp;
Both were gradually running out of supplies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:39.600 --> 00:28:45.520
In October, Gladwin wrote to Amherst:&nbsp;
“The enemy are masters of the country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:45.520 --> 00:28:52.800
the season far advanced, not a stick of firewood&nbsp;
in the garrison, and but little provisions.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:53.840 --> 00:28:58.960
The besiegers were faring little better, and&nbsp;
the stalemate of the siege caused Pontiac’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:58.960 --> 00:29:06.560
influence among the warriors to wane. As a cold&nbsp;
wind heralded the coming of winter, more and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:06.560 --> 00:29:14.320
warriors left his camp to prepare for the cold&nbsp;
season. On October 31, he finally had to admit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:14.320 --> 00:29:21.200
that the French would not be coming. He lifted the&nbsp;
siege and moved south across the Maumee River to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:21.200 --> 00:29:27.440
continue the resistance against the British there.&nbsp;
Fort Detroit remained in British hands for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:27.440 --> 00:29:34.240
time being—but the war was not yet over.
